(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-cell-physiology-membranes-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes — Physiology Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does the lipid bilayer act as, functionally?","choices":["A barrier to water and water-soluble substances","A repository of genetic information for the cell","The primary site of ATP synthesis in the cell","A pump that requires ATP for every type of transport"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lipid bilayer acts as a barrier to water and to substances that dissolve in water, though specific molecules can still cross via membrane proteins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Roughly how many different proteins involved in transport do cell membranes contain?","choices":["About 50","Fewer than 10","Over 300","Over 10,000"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cell membranes contain over 300 different proteins that play a role in transport, providing specificity and function (ion channels, carrier proteins). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the key difference between diffusion and active transport across the cell membrane?","choices":["Diffusion requires direct ATP hydrolysis to proceed; active transport occurs spontaneously down its gradient without energy","Active transport only occurs during the mitotic phase of the cell cycle, never during interphase","Diffusion occurs down a concentration gradient with no additional energy required; active transport occurs against a gradient and requires ATP","They are simply two different names describing the exact same passive transport process across membranes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Diffusion occurs down a concentration gradient and requires no additional energy, while active transport occurs against a concentration gradient and requires energy (ATP), using a protein carrier. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What distinguishes facilitated diffusion from simple diffusion?","choices":["There is no meaningful difference between the two; both terms simply describe passive movement of any dissolved molecule down its concentration gradient without any protein involvement","Simple diffusion only occurs in muscle cells, where lipid-soluble hormones cross the sarcolemma directly without any channel or carrier proteins","Facilitated diffusion requires ATP directly, hydrolyzing one molecule of ATP for each solute molecule transported across the membrane","Facilitated diffusion involves water-soluble molecules crossing via protein channels/transport proteins, while simple diffusion is for lipid-soluble molecules crossing directly through the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Simple diffusion allows lipid-soluble molecules to move readily across the membrane based on lipid solubility, while facilitated diffusion allows water-soluble molecules to cross via protein channels and other transport proteins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What do aquaglyceroporins transport, in addition to water?","choices":["Glycerol, urea, and ammonia","Only calcium and magnesium ions","Only glucose and fructose molecules","Only sodium and potassium ions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aquaglyceroporins are a subset of aquaporins that transport glycerol, urea, and ammonia across cell membranes, in addition to water. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What are the two main types of ion channels?","choices":["Aquaporins and aquaglyceroporins, both selective for water and small solutes","Primary active transporters and secondary active transporters, both requiring ATP","Ungated channels and gated channels (voltage-gated or chemical/ligand-gated)","Symporters and antiporters, both classified as secondary active transport carriers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ion channels are either ungated (transport determined by size, shape, and charge) or gated — either voltage-gated (e.g., voltage-gated Na+ channels) or chemical/ligand-gated (e.g., nicotinic acetylcholine receptor channels). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How can the potassium channel be permeable to potassium but not to sodium, even though the hydrated sodium ion is smaller than expected?","choices":["Sodium ions are actively pumped out by a separate ATP-dependent exclusion pump located directly adjacent to the potassium channel's pore, before they ever reach the selectivity filter","Carbonyl oxygens in the selectivity filter strip water molecules from potassium (but not sodium), so only the dehydrated potassium ion (which is actually smaller than dehydrated... larger than hydrated) can permeate","Potassium channels are physically blocked to sodium by a rigid protein plug that only the potassium ion is small enough to displace during channel gating","Sodium carries no net charge once dissolved in physiological solution, and therefore cannot pass through any gated or ungated ion channel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbonyl oxygens lining the potassium channel selectivity filter strip water molecules from the potassium ion (but not sodium), allowing the dehydrated potassium ion to permeate while the differently-hydrated sodium ion cannot. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What allows the sodium channel to selectively conduct sodium over the physically larger (hydrated) potassium ion?","choices":["Potassium is repelled by a ring of positively charged lysine residues lining the channel pore, unlike sodium, which is instead attracted inward","Sodium channels have no true selectivity mechanism at all, allowing any similarly-charged cation to pass through the pore freely","Negatively charged amino acids (glutamate) lining the channel pull the sodium ion away from its water shell, allowing the smaller unhydrated sodium ion to diffuse through, while unhydrated potassium is too large","Sodium channels are only open transiently during the mitotic phase of the cell cycle, remaining closed at all other times"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sodium channels are lined with negatively charged amino acids (glutamate) that pull the sodium ion away from its water shell, letting the smaller unhydrated sodium ion diffuse through, while the unhydrated potassium ion is too large to pass. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What limits the maximum rate (Vmax) of facilitated diffusion for a given protein transporter?","choices":["The temperature of the surrounding cytosol only, independent of transporter number","The maximum rate of conformational change of the protein transporter","The concentration of ATP available to power the transporter directly","The total number of ribosomes actively synthesizing new transporter proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"The maximum rate of facilitated (carrier-mediated) diffusion for a given protein transporter is limited by the maximum rate of conformational change of that transporter, not simply by the number of carriers present. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does a hydrostatic pressure difference affect net diffusion across a membrane?","choices":["Pressure has no measurable effect on diffusion rate across any biological membrane whatsoever","Higher pressure results in increased energy available to cause net movement from high to low pressure","Higher pressure always stops net diffusion entirely, regardless of the concentration gradient present","Pressure only affects active, ATP-dependent transport, never passive diffusion across the membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"A hydrostatic pressure difference is one factor affecting net diffusion rate — higher pressure results in increased energy available to cause net movement from the high-pressure side to the low-pressure side. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the difference between primary and secondary active transport?","choices":["Both primary and secondary active transport directly hydrolyze ATP molecules to pump their respective solutes across the membrane","Primary active transport only moves potassium ions across the membrane, never sodium, calcium, or hydrogen ions","Secondary active transport requires no energy at all, since it simply moves solutes freely down their own independent gradient","Primary active transport directly uses ATP energy to pump molecules against a gradient; secondary active transport indirectly uses the energy stored in another molecule's electrochemical gradient (usually Na+)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Primary active transport directly uses ATP energy to pump molecules against an electrochemical gradient, while secondary active transport indirectly uses energy stored in the electrochemical gradient of another molecule (usually Na+). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What does the Na+-K+ ATPase pump do, and what happens if it is inhibited (e.g., by ouabain)?","choices":["It has no role in maintaining cellular osmotic balance, functioning instead only to generate nerve signals","It pumps sodium out of cells and potassium into cells against their gradients; inhibition (e.g., by ouabain) causes the cell to swell and burst","It only functions within neurons and glial cells, playing no role whatsoever in other cell types","It pumps sodium into cells and potassium out against their gradients; inhibition causes the cell to shrink"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps sodium out of cells and potassium into cells against their electrochemical gradients, playing a critical role in osmotic balance — inhibiting the pump (e.g., with ouabain) causes the cell to swell and burst. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the transport stoichiometry of the Na+-K+ ATPase, and how electrogenic is it?","choices":["3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in; electrogenic, but contributes less than 10% to the membrane potential","1 Na+ out for 1 K+ in; completely non-electrogenic, contributing 0% to the membrane potential","4 Na+ out for 4 K+ in; non-electrogenic, but contributes 50% of the resting membrane potential","2 Na+ out for 3 K+ in; highly electrogenic, contributing most of the membrane potential"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase transports 3 Na+ out for every 2 K+ in, making it electrogenic — but this electrogenic nature contributes less than 10% to the resting membrane potential. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where is H+ ATPase found, and what is one of its functions?","choices":["In parietal cells of gastric glands (HCl secretion) and intercalated cells of renal tubules (controlling blood pH), concentrating H+ ions up to a million-fold","In red blood cells only, regulating hemoglobin's oxygen-binding affinity directly during transport","Only in mitochondria, driving ATP synthesis via the electron transport chain's inner membrane proton gradient","Only in the nucleus, regulating chromatin condensation through localized pH changes near DNA"],"answer":0,"exp":"H+ ATPase is found in parietal cells of gastric glands (secreting HCl) and intercalated cells of renal tubules (controlling blood pH), and can concentrate H+ ions up to a million-fold. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a symporter, in the context of secondary active transport?","choices":["A transporter that moves a substance in the opposite direction of a driver ion","A transporter that moves a substance in the same direction as a \"driver\" ion like Na+","A channel that requires no driver ion, moving substances passively down their own gradient","A pump that directly hydrolyzes ATP to move a substance against its own gradient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Symporters transport a substance in the same direction as a \"driver\" ion like Na+, while antiporters transport a substance in the opposite direction of the driver ion — both are protein cotransporters using an electrochemical gradient (usually sodium's). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is osmosis?","choices":["The net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane, from an area of high water concentration to an area of low water concentration","The semiconservative replication of DNA prior to cell division during the S phase of the cell cycle","The sequential movement and modification of proteins through the compartments of the Golgi apparatus","The ATP-dependent active transport of ions against their own concentration gradient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Osmosis is the net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane, moving from an area of high water concentration to an area of low water concentration. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How is osmotic pressure defined?","choices":["The pressure exerted only by dissolved plasma proteins circulating within the vascular compartment","The pressure that must be applied to the solution side to stop fluid movement when a semipermeable membrane separates a solution from pure water","The total hydrostatic pressure measured inside a living cell at any given resting moment","The pressure required to speed up water movement across a membrane, applied to the pure-water side instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Osmotic pressure is the pressure that must be applied to the solution side to stop fluid movement when a semipermeable membrane separates a solution from pure water, attributed to the solution's osmolarity. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What determines the osmolarity of a solution — the mass of solute, or something else?","choices":["The temperature only, independent of any solute concentration present","The total mass of the solute dissolved, regardless of particle number","The solute particle density (number of particles), not the mass of the solute","The color of the solution, as determined by any dissolved pigments present"],"answer":2,"exp":"The osmolarity of a solution is a function of solute particle density (number of particles per liter), not the mass of the solute — a solution of a low-molecular-weight solute can have far more particles (and thus higher osmolarity) than an equal mass of a high-molecular-weight solute. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"150 mM NaCl and 300 mM glucose both equal approximately what osmolarity, and why?","choices":["0 mOsm/L for both, since neither solute is considered capable of dissociating in aqueous solution","They cannot be meaningfully compared, since molarity and osmolarity measure entirely unrelated solution properties","300 mOsm/L, because NaCl dissociates into two particles (Na+ and Cl-) per molecule while glucose does not dissociate","150 mOsm/L, because glucose does not dissociate while NaCl also remains fully associated in solution"],"answer":2,"exp":"150 mM NaCl dissociates into 2 particles per molecule (Na+ and Cl-), yielding 300 mOsm/L, matching 300 mM glucose (which does not dissociate) at 300 mOsm/L — both represent 300 mOsm/L. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which of the following is classified as a permeant molecule (crosses the membrane almost as fast as water)?","choices":["Free amino acids","Charged sodium ions","Urea and glycerol","Glucose molecules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Permeant molecules like urea and glycerol have relatively high lipid solubility and move through the cell membrane almost as fast as water, unlike impermeant molecules such as ions, glucose, and amino acids. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What determines steady-state cell volume?","choices":["The concentration of permeant particles like urea, dissolved within the extracellular fluid","The total number of mitochondria present within the cytoplasm of a given cell","The concentration of impermeant particles in the extracellular fluid (e.g., Na+, K+, proteins)","The rate of DNA replication occurring during the S phase of the cell division cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Steady-state cell volume depends on the concentration of impermeant particles in the extracellular fluid (Na+, K+, proteins); permeant particles like urea and glycerol cause only transient changes in cell volume. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the key difference between tonicity and osmolarity?","choices":["Osmolarity depends on the membrane's permeability characteristics; tonicity does not depend on the membrane at all","Tonicity only applies to dissolved gases within the extracellular fluid compartment","Osmolarity is simply a measure of solute concentration; tonicity (effective osmotic pressure) depends on both the membrane and the solute's permeability","They are identical concepts that both depend solely on the total solute particle concentration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Osmolarity is merely another way of expressing the concentration of a solution, while tonicity (effective osmotic pressure) depends on the properties of both the membrane and the solute — specifically its permeability. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Roughly what fraction of a typical cell's energy — and up to what fraction of a neuron's energy — does the Na+-K+ ATPase require?","choices":["About 1/100 of any cell's energy, up to 1/10 of a neuron's energy","None — the pump runs passively with no energy cost","About 1/5 of a typical cell's energy, up to 2/3 of a neuron's energy","Nearly all of a cell's energy budget, up to 9/10 of a neuron's energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase requires about 1/5 of a typical cell's energy budget and up to 2/3 of a neuron's energy, reflecting how metabolically demanding maintaining ionic gradients is, especially in excitable cells. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Is 300 mOsm/L urea isotonic to a typical cell (300 mOsm/L)?","choices":["No, because 300 mOsm/L urea is not actually isosmotic to the cell's own 300 mOsm/L interior","Yes, because it is isosmotic, and any isosmotic solution is by definition also isotonic","No — although it is isosmotic, urea is a permeant solute that crosses into the cell, so it is not isotonic","Yes, because urea never crosses cell membranes, behaving as a fully impermeant solute like sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"300 mOsm/L urea is isosmotic (same osmolarity as the cell) but not isotonic — because urea is a permeant solute, it crosses into the cell rather than exerting sustained osmotic pressure, so the cell will swell and burst. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A cell (300 mOsm/L) is placed in a solution of 300 mOsm/L glycerol plus 200 mOsm/L NaCl. What happens to cell volume?","choices":["The cell shrinks and stays shrunken","No net change occurs at all","The cell swells and stays swollen","The cell shrinks then swells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because the impermeant NaCl (200 mOsm/L) is initially lower than the cell's 300 mOsm/L, the cell first shrinks; but the permeant glycerol equilibrates into the cell over time, causing it to subsequently swell. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A cell (300 mOsm/L) is placed in 200 mOsm/L urea. What happens to cell volume?","choices":["Shrinks then swells","Shrinks and stays shrunk","No change occurs","Swells and bursts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because urea is a permeant solute, the cell in 200 mOsm/L urea swells and bursts as urea and water move into the cell. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What ionic condition is required for motile cilia to produce their whiplike movements (in addition to ATP)?","choices":["Complete absence of sodium and potassium ions","Appropriate ionic conditions, e.g., Ca2+","High extracellular osmolarity only","High extracellular glucose concentration alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motile cilia movement requires ATP and appropriate ionic conditions, such as Ca2+, similar to the flagellum of sperm. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Approximately how many times does water in a red blood cell exchange during its transport through a capillary?","choices":["1000 times","10 times","1 time","100 times"],"answer":3,"exp":"Water in a red blood cell exchanges approximately 100 times during its transport through a capillary, reflecting the astounding rapidity of water diffusion through aquaporins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In transcellular transport of glucose and amino acids across an epithelial cell, what drives the initial entry of glucose/AA from the lumen into the cell?","choices":["Osmotic pressure alone, independent of any sodium ion gradient","Secondary active transport coupled to the Na+ gradient (Na+ co-transport)","Direct ATP hydrolysis by the glucose transporter itself","Simple diffusion alone with no ion involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucose and amino acid entry from the intestinal/renal lumen into epithelial cells is driven by secondary active transport, coupled to the Na+ electrochemical gradient (Na+/glucose and Na+/AA symporters). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What does the existence of molecular concentration gradients across the cell membrane primarily reflect?","choices":["The complete absence of any membrane transport proteins, allowing free unrestricted diffusion of every molecule","Purely random thermodynamic chance, with no biological significance or underlying regulatory mechanism","Equal concentrations of every molecule maintained identically inside and outside the cell membrane","The selective permeability and active transport mechanisms of the membrane, maintaining different intracellular vs extracellular ion/molecule concentrations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Molecular gradients across the cell membrane (e.g., different concentrations of Na+, K+, and other solutes inside vs. outside the cell) reflect the membrane's selective permeability and active transport mechanisms. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
